  "notes": "Measured 2026-11-24 for ghost flights on 727-200 N456BN (November 1984, fitted to shapes/B722.json); checked on N466BN, N447BN, N467BN (right side), a Jon Proctor photo of December 1984 and N275WC in January 1987. No dated 1985 photo was found on Commons; the 1984 and 1987 photos show the same scheme and Braniff kept it until 1988, when some aircraft got a revival of the earlier Harper and George scheme (not drawn). Every edge is a fitted straight line, not a trace: the blue's top is level 1.11 m below the centerline (within 0.06 m of the photo's along the cabin; the photo's slight rise aft is the fit's tilt, the pewter line's shows the same), the pewter line 0.32 m deep over it, and the tail's diagonal one straight line rising 0.643 m per meter aft (32.7 degrees), within 0.01 m of the photo from the side engine's exhaust to the tailplane's shadow, sampled at the outline's own points so it draws straight. Where it meets the belly line (33.7 m back, under the side engine) is hidden behind the nacelle in every photo; the corner is where the two lines cross. The diagonal runs on into the tailplane fairing's aft end, which is blue on N275WC; the line meets it in shadow on the 1984 photos. The fin's paint is carried down over the S-duct's fairing, where the renderer draws fin space straight from the root to two spans below it: the diagonal's one point there is placed to keep it within 0.02 m of straight over the fairing. The fuselage title's letters dip into the top of the pewter line on N456BN. Registrations (small gray, aft of the wing) and the nose-gear door's fleet number aren't drawn; no US flag shows on any photo. Colors are read off slides (N456BN's is dark and red-shifted) and are approximate; the blue is near black on most photos. The restarted Braniff (Braniff, Inc., 1984-1989, Dallas/Fort Worth then Kansas City) is keyed BN1984 to tell it from Braniff International (1928-1982), which used the same code. Its 1985 fleet was all 727-200s; 737-200s came in 1987. Refitted 2026-11-24 on the passenger 727 (shapes/B722-passenger.json, its cabin level): the pewter line's top 2.89 m and the blue's 3.19 m under the crown (N456BN, clear of the title), the diagonals at their measured 32.7 degrees from the same corner. The title already sat as on N456BN (its foot in the pewter, 0.1 m under the windows).",
